# The annotations of ZYNAPS.B's sector 1: the Hrust 1 depacker (256 bytes at $5B00), the single source of
# src/loader/depack*.asm (tools/part_mkasm.py). The image is work/parts/depacker-5b00.bin.
#
# The code is what the analysis builds work/stage2/loader (block 1's unpack, recording c1 frames 2051-2183) and
# work/stage2/load2 (block 2's, frames 2838-3302) ran. The format it reads is tools/hrust1.py's docstring; both blocks
# unpack there with 0 bytes different from the recording (docs/03-build-and-load.md 3.3).

@equ $2758 ROM_HL_ALT ; the value the 48 BASIC ROM keeps in HL' (the calculator's): restored before the return

@module depack ; the Hrust 1 depacker (Dmitry Pyankov's HR format), unpacking in place
@about The loader reads it to $5B00 and enters it by JP with HL = the packed block (its "HR" header) and DE = the destination; here both are $6000. The return address is the loader's: Depack ends with a RET.
@about The block is first moved up so that its last byte lies on the last byte of the destination (LDDR), then unpacked forward from the destination: the output never overtakes the input. The six last bytes of the data, which the header holds apart, are put in a buffer under the stack (IX) and written by the end code as three words; the end code also points SP back at the return address.
@about Two register sets. The main set: DE the output, HL the source of a copy, BC its length. The alternate set: HL the bit word (bits leave through ADD HL,HL, MSB first), B the bits left in it, C = 16, D the window's marker byte (its 0 bit says how many high bits a long distance has: 2 at the start, one more after each $FE escape), E the length being built. Bit groups are read into A with a marker: A starts with a pattern whose first 0 bit, shifted out by RLA, ends the loop after the wanted number of bits.
@about The tokens are those of tools/hrust1.py: flag 1 a literal; flag 0 then a length code (2-bit groups, 1 + 3 for each 11) and a distance code; 11 00 opens the special codes (an X ? X with a short distance, a run of literal words, a long length, the end).
$5B00 c Depack ; unpack the HR block at HL to DE in place, return to the caller's caller
> Input: HL = the packed block (header "HR", the unpacked length, the packed length, the last 6 bytes, the stream), DE = the destination. Output: the data at DE; SP back where it was; HL' = ROM_HL_ALT for BASIC. Uses every register, IX, the 12 bytes under the stack.
$5B00 ; IX: 12 bytes under the stack (the destination, then the last 6 bytes)
$5B06 ; the destination at IX+10
$5B07 ; read the header through SP: "HR", the lengths
$5B0A ; the unpacked length - 1: DE = the last byte of the destination
$5B0E ; the packed length - 1: HL = the last byte of the block
$5B11 ; the block's end below the destination's end: move it up there; else it stays
$5B16 ; no move: copy the block onto itself
$5B18 MoveBlock ; the block to the top of the destination, from its end down
$5B1B ; DE = the destination again
$5B21 ; SP on the moved block: skip "HR" and the two lengths
$5B25 ; the 6 last bytes to IX+6..IX+11, one byte a DEC SP / POP AF
$5B27 SaveLast6
$5B30 ; the bit reader: D = the window's marker (2 bits of high distance), B = C = 16, the first word
$5B37 Literal ; a byte from the stream (DEC SP / POP AF takes one byte), to the output
$5B3A PutByte ; the byte in A to the output
$5B3C NextToken ; back to the bit reader's set
$5B3D TokenFlag ; the next bit: 1 a literal, 0 a match
$5B42 TokenFlagIn
$5B44 Match ; E = 1: the length is built from 2-bit groups
$5B46 LengthGroup ; two bits into A (the marker $80)
$5B48 LengthBit
$5B4D LengthBitIn
$5B50 ; 0-2 ends the code; 3 adds 3 to E and reads another group, up to 16
$5B59 LengthDone ; A = the length code: 4 (11 00) opens the special codes
$5B5E ; past 4 the code is the length + 1
$5B60 ; length 1: a 3-bit distance; length 2: its own distance codes
$5B63 SetLength ; C = the length (the main set)
$5B64 Distance ; the distance's code: two bits under the marker $BF
$5B69 DistanceBit
$5B6E DistanceBitIn
$5B71 ; length 2: Len2Distance
$5B73 ; length 3 and more: 00 -> $FE (-512 + a byte), 01 -> $FF (-256 + a byte), 10 -> 5 bits, 11 -> the window's high bits
$5B78 Len2Distance ; length 2: 00 / 01 / 10 -> high byte $FD / $FE / $FF and a low byte; 11 -> 5 bits
$5B7B ; a 5-bit distance (the marker $F7 after RRCA): -32..-1
$5B7D ShortDistance ; length 1 comes here with $BF: RRCA makes $DF, 3 bits, -8..-1
$5B7F HighBits ; bits under the marker in A: the short distance, or the high byte under the window's marker D
$5B84 HighBitsIn
$5B87 SetHigh ; H = the high byte ($FF for a short distance), L = the low byte: the copy's source
$5B8C ; a high byte of $FF: the low byte may be an escape
$5B91 ; the low byte from the stream
$5B92 CopyMatch ; HL = DE + the negative distance; C bytes copied
$5B96 NextTokenJump
$5B98 GrowWindow ; the $FE escape: the window's marker D rotated, one more high bit for long distances
$5B9D ByteDistance ; a low byte with high byte $FF: below $E0 a distance -256..-33, else an X ? X
$5BA2 ; rol(b) xor the length (2 or 3), + 1: $FE with length 2 grows the window
$5BA7 ; the X ? X distance
$5BA9 InsertCopy ; X ? X: one byte from the distance, one literal, one byte from the distance
$5BB8 Special ; 11 00: 1 -> an X ? X with a 4-bit distance; 01 -> a run of literal words; 00 -> a 7-bit length code
$5BBA SpecialBit
$5BBF SpecialBitIn
$5BC4 ; 00: seven bits (the marker $FC)
$5BC8 LongLength ; a code below 15: the length is code * 256 + a byte from the stream
$5BCF LengthOrEnd ; the 7-bit code: below 15 a long length, 15 the end, above it the length itself
$5BD5 ; the end: A = 3 words, SP on the six saved bytes
$5BDB FourBits ; four bits (the marker $EF): an X ? X distance -16..-1, or the run's count
$5BDE FourBit
$5BE3 FourBitIn
$5BE9 ; a run of literals: (v + 6) words
$5BEF CopyWords ; A words from SP to the output (a run from the stream, or the six saved bytes at the end)
$5BF0 CopyWord
$5BF9 ; not the end: the next token
$5BFB ; the end: HL' as BASIC wants it; RET from the restored SP to the caller
